<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A sheet whose data stops at row 10 and whose fills do not, arranged so that the *start* of the
  attribute scan is the only thing that can produce the right answer.

  Calc asks each column for its last visibly attributed row and passes that column's OWN last
  data row into the question — "always including notes, 0 if none", ScColumn::GetLastVisibleAttr
  (sc/inc/column.hxx:892-897). A column holding no data is therefore scanned from the top of the
  sheet, not from wherever the sheet's data happens to end, and the run lengths that decide
  whether the column prints are measured from row 1 rather than from row 11.

  Column C is filled on rows 1 to 84 and holds nothing. Measured from row 1 that is a run of
  exactly 83 rows — ScAttrArray::GetLastVisibleAttr clamps the run's start to nLastData + 1
  (attarray.cxx:1961), so the first row is not in the sum — which is one row inside
  SC_VISATTR_STOP, so Calc takes it and the print area reaches row 84. Measured from row 0 it is
  84 and the scan stops with nothing.

  Column E is filled on rows 1 to 100, and its row 1 differs from the rest by a border. Row 1 on
  its own is a run of zero rows by the same clamp, so Calc takes it and keeps the column; the 99
  rows below it are one run past the limit and end the scan. Measured from the sheet's last data
  row instead, everything Calc actually used is above the start and column E is dropped.

  So the printed area is A1:E84, and the three ways of getting the start wrong each break it
  differently: from the sheet's last data row loses column E, dropping the column's first row
  loses column E as well, and starting an empty column at row 0 rather than at Calc's row 1
  loses column C and with it row 84.

  Taken from "Computer and Software Services_50 State Comparison.xlsx", whose columns I to O
  carry a fill on all 129 rows and no data, and whose print area therefore stopped at column H
  where Calc's reaches O — a whole third column band, 24 pages against 26.
-->
